ppc64le-v8-int32-overflow-cmpd.patch -- WITHDRAWN 2026-10-17, shipped briefly in
chromium 153.0.8010.52-7 and produces SILENT WRONG ARITHMETIC.

The patch replaced the XER overflow check for kPPC_Add32/kPPC_Sub32 with
"add; extsw; cmpd; bne", on the stated premise that the backend keeps every
Word32 value sign-extended in its 64-bit register. That premise is FALSE.
instruction-selector-ppc.cc maps MemoryRepresentation::Int32()/Uint32() and
MachineRepresentation::kWord32 loads to kPPC_LoadWordU32, i.e. lwz, which
ZERO-extends; lwa is used only for compressed pointers. slw/srw/rlwinm also
zero-extend. So the upper word of a Word32 is simply undefined, which is why
upstream's Tst32 register form carries its own extsw. and why Maglev's ppc port
zero-extends.

Consequences, reproduced on the shipped -7 binary:

  const neg = new Int32Array([-2147483648]);
  f = (a,i,x) => a[i] + x;          // warmed with positive loads only
  f(neg, 0, -1)  ->  2147483647     // correct answer: -2147483649

  lwz(INT32_MIN) = 0x0000000080000000; addi -1 -> 0x000000007fffffff;
  extsw(0x7fffffff) == 0x7fffffff, so no overflow is flagged, while addo
  reports OV32=1.

The mirror case -- a loaded negative operand whose sum is not its own sign
extension -- deopts spuriously on every checked add, which is merely slow.

The original correctness test passed 1362 checks because it used literal
constants and Smi arrays; Smi loads go through srawi, which sign-extends. It
never loaded a negative int32 from an Int32Array. Any replacement MUST include
that case.

An exact fix inside the cmpd idea needs an explicit extsw of each register
input first, which costs the +1/+2 instructions the patch existed to remove.
The XER path and the POWER8 sign-XOR synthesis are both immune and remain
correct.

NOT affected and still valid: the same extsw/cmpd identity applied to
kPPC_Mul32, because mullw reads only the low 32 bits of its operands and
ignores the upper words entirely.
